Qxd2 Nxd2 12. Kxd2 Kxd8 { bkm: PRIO_1 }) 8... Be7 9. Nxe7 (9. Bxf6 Bxf6 10. e3 dxe3 11. Nxe3 O-O 12. Be2 Nc6 { e4 is fine already, but it's even stronger if white castles first which is common. } 13. O-O e4 14. Nd2 Bxb2 15. Rb1 Be5 16. Nxe4 Qh4 17. Ng3 f5 { White can't stop f4 in a good way. Qd5+ is not a problem, we simply step aside with Kh8.  @@StartCAL@@%cal Gf5f4,Gf4f3,Gf4g3,Gf4e3@@EndCAL@@ }) 9... Qxe7 10. e3 Bg4 11. Be2 d3 12. Qxd3 e4 13. Bxf6 gxf6 14. Qd4 Nc6 15. Qc3 exf3) 6... Nxd5 7. Nxd5 Qxd5 8. e3 Be7 9. Bb5+ Bd7 10. Bxd7+ Nxd7 11. O-O O-O 12. b3 Rac8 13. Bb2 Rfd8 { Next we can play Qe4, and aim for Nd5. Completely equal position. }) (3. g3 { 8% } 3... cxd4 4. Nxd4 d5 5. Bg2 e5 6. Nf3 Nc6 7. O-O Be7 8. c4 d4 9. e3 O-O 10. exd4 exd4 11. Bf4 Bf5 12. Nbd2 Re8 13. Re1 Nb4) (3. Bf4 { 6% } 3... cxd4 4. Nxd4 { 51% } (4. Qxd4 { 36% } 4... d6 5. Qd1 e5 6. Bg3 Qb6 7. b3 Nc6 8. e3 Bf5 9. Be2 Nb4 10. Na3 Qa5) 4... e5) 3... b5 { c4 is the most common move - transposes into benko. } 4. Bg5 { 22% } (4. a4 { 20% } 4... b4 5. c4 g6 6. b3 Bg7 7. Bb2 O-O 8. Nbd2 e6 9. e4 exd5 10. exd5 Re8+ 11. Be2 Qe7 12. Kf1 d6 13. Bd3 Nxd5 14. Bxg7 Nf4 15. Be4 d5 16. Bxd5 { White is more likely to capture with the pawn, which is a mistake: } 16... Nxd5 { White shouldn't try to save the bishop: } 17. cxd5 Kxg7 18. Nc4 Nd7) (4. e4 { 7% } 4... Nxe4 5. Bd3 Nf6 { We could've tried to hold to the pawn, but it looks very messy. } 6. Bxb5 g6 7. O-O Bg7 8. d6 { 30% } (8. c4 { 27% } 8... O-O 9. Nc3 d6 10. Re1 a6 11. Ba4 Nbd7 12. Qe2 Nb6 13. Bc6 Rb8 14. b3 Nfxd5) 8... O-O 9. dxe7 Qxe7 10. Re1 Qd8 11. Nc3 Bb7 12. Bg5 d5 13. Ne5 d4 14. Ne4 Bxe4 15. Rxe4 Qb6) 4... g6 5. Bxf6 { 77% } (5. d6 { 4% } 5... exd6 6. Nc3 Be7 7. Nxb5 Qa5+ 8. Nc3 d5 9. e3 h6 10. Bf4 Nc6 11. Be2 d6 12. O-O Be6) 5... exf6 6. e4 b4 7. Nbd2 { 51% } (7. a3 { 17% } 7... Bg7 8. axb4 cxb4 9. Nbd2 O-O 10. Bd3 a5 11. O-O f5 12. exf5 Bxb2 13. fxg6 hxg6 14. Ra2 Bg7 { Next we can play d6 and try Nd7 -> Nc5. }) 7... Bg7 8. Bd3 d6 { O-O is fine, but we start with d6 to avoid branches. Now white can't play d6 themselves. } 9. O-O O-O 10. Nc4 Qc7 11. a3 a5 12. axb4 cxb4 13. Nd4 Bd7 14. c3 bxc3 15. bxc3 a4 { Look to play Na6 and Rfb8 next.  Example continuation } 16. Ra3 Na6 17. Nb2 Nc5 18. Bc2 Rfb8) (2. b3 { 6% } 2... d5 3. Bb2 Bf5 4. e3 h6 5. Be2 Nbd7 6. O-O e6 7. c4 Bd6 8. Nc3 O-O 9. Rc1 c6 { Possible continuation: } 10. d4 Qe7 11. Bd3 Rfe8) 2... d5 3. cxd5 (3. g3 { 14% } 3... dxc4 4. Bg2 { 69% } (4. Qa4+ { 17% } 4... Nc6 5. Qxc4 { 61% } (5. Bg2 { 35% } 5... Nd7 6. Qxc4 e5 7. O-O Be7 8. d3 { If white goes d4 instead: } 8... Nb6 9. Qc2 O-O 10. Nc3 Be6 11. a3 Nd4 12. Nxd4 exd4 13. Ne4 Bd5 14. Bf4 c5 15. Nxc5 Bxg2 16. Kxg2 Qd5+ 17. Ne4 f5) 5... e5 6. Bg2 Be6 7. Qc2 { 41% } (7. Qb5 { 34% } 7... a6 8. Qxb7 Na5 { A beautiful queen trap. }) (7. Qa4 { 21% } 7... Nd7 8. Nc3 Nc5 9. Qc2 Nb4 10. Qb1 Ncd3+ 11. exd3 Nxd3+ 12. Kf1 Bc5 13. Ne4 Bb6 14. Qc2 f5 15. Neg5 Bg8 16. a4 e4) 7... Qd7 8. O-O h5 9. h4 e4 10. Ng5 Nd4 11. Qd1 O-O-O { We're not afraid of Nxe5 - we'd simply go for the attack: } 12. Nxe4 Nxe4 13. Bxe4 g5 14. e3 gxh4 15. exd4 hxg3 16. Nc3 gxf2+ 17. Rxf2 Rg8+) (4. Na3 { 8% This idea doesn't work as well this early. } 4... e5 5. Nxe5 Qd5 6. Nf3 Bxa3 7. bxa3 Bf5 8. Bg2 Nc6 9. O-O Be4 10. Bb2 O-O-O 11. Qa4 a5 12. Rfc1 b5 13. Qd1 Rhe8 14. d3 cxd3 15. exd3 Qxd3 16. Qxd3 Rxd3 17. Ne1 Rd6) 4... Be6 { Ugly looking move - but actually the top choice of the fish. } 5. O-O { 60% } (5. Ng5 { 8% } 5... Bd5 6. e4 Bc6 7. O-O h6 { White is in an awkward position - Move the knight and we'll grab on e4. Playing e5 is most common, a blunder, as we can simply play Bxg2 and the knight is still hanging. }) 5... a5 { A fish novelty that hasn't been played in this position before. One idea is to provoke a4 and get an outpost via Na6 -> Nb4. } 6. Nc3 c6 7. a4 { We provoke white into creating an outpost on b4! } (7. Re1 Na6 { It looks like we want to play b5, but we don't! It's better to keep the pawn chain. Look to fianchetto the bishop next. } 8. Ng5 Bd7 9. b3 cxb3 10. Qxb3 e6 11. Qxb7 Nb4 12. Rb1 Bc5 13. Nge4 Rb8 14. Nxc5 Rxb7 15. Nxb7 Qc7 16. Nc5 O-O { We're 'up' a queen for rook and a bishop - but the position is still only -0.5~ for black. Important moves to look at next are Nc2, Qe5 and e5. We don't mind trading. }) 7... Na6 { Next we can look to play Nb4 and fianchetto our bishop. If white plays Ng5 poking our bishop, we can play Bf5 since white doesn't have any attacker on c5. }) (3. e3 { 12% White chickens out - will likely transpose into a colle setup but with a pawn on c4. } 3... e6 4. Nc3 b6 5. b3 { 36% If white starts with b3, we start with Bb7. Otherwise we can start with Bd6. } (5. cxd5 { 26% } 5... exd5 6. d4 Bd6 7. Bd3 Bb7 8. O-O O-O 9. Re1 Re8 10. Qc2 c5 11. dxc5 bxc5 12. Bb5 Nbd7) (5. d4 { 21% } 5... Bd6 { In case of d4, we go Bd6 before Bb7. } 6. Bd3 Bb7 7. O-O O-O 8. Re1 dxc4 9. Bxc4 c5 10. e4 cxd4 11. Nxd4 Be5 12. Ndb5 Qc8) 5... Bb7 6. Bb2 Nbd7 { When white gets to develop their bishop and knight like this, we need to prepare Bd6 with Nbd7 and a6. } 7. Be2 a6 { If white has a knight on c3 - we prepare Bd6 with a6. } 8. O-O Bd6 9. d4 O-O 10. Rc1 Qe7 11. Qc2) (3. d4 { 10% } 3... e6 4. Nc3 Be7 { Bad luck, we've transposed into a boring QGD. This is probably not part of white's main repertoire however, as they didn't start with 1.d4. }) (3. b3 { 5% } 3... dxc4 4. bxc4 e5 5. Nxe5 { 34% } (5. Bb2 { 26% } 5... e4 6. Nd4 Bd6 7. g3 Be5 8. e3 { 100% } (8. Qa4+ { Only move that survives for white. e3 walks into c5. } 8... Nbd7 9. Qa3 Bd6 10. Qc3 O-O) 8... c5 9. Ne6 Qd6 10. Nxg7+ Kf8 { Next we'll get the knight. }) 5... Qd4 6. Qa4+ c6 7. Nxf7 Qxa1 8. Nxh8 Qxb1) 3... c6 4. dxc6 (4. d4 { 9% } 4... cxd5 { White 'tricked' us into a boring slav exchange variation. } 5. Nc3 Nc6 6. Bf4 { 48% } (6. Bg5 Ne4 7. Bf4 (7. Bh4 { 24% } 7... Qa5 8. Qb3 e5 9. e3 { 62% } (9. Qxd5 { 23% } 9... Qb4 10. Qxe4 Qxb2 11. Rd1 Qxc3+ 12. Nd2 Qxd4) 9... Bb4 10. Rc1 O-O 11. Be2 exd4 12. Nxd4 Nxd4 13. exd4 Nxc3 14. bxc3 Ba3 15. Rd1 Re8 16. Rd2 Bg4 17. f3 Bd7 18. O-O) 7... e6 8. e3 g5 9. Bg3 h5 10. h4 (10. Nxe4 { 21% } 10... dxe4 11. Nd2 h4 12. Be5 f6) 10... Nxg3 11. fxg3 g4 12. Nd2 Bh6 13. Qe2 O-O 14. a3 e5 15. dxe5 d4) 6... Nh5 7. Bg3 { 27% } (7. Bg5 { 26% } 7... h6 8. Bh4 g5 9. Bg3 Nxg3 10. hxg3 g4 11. Ne5 Bg7 12. Nxc6 bxc6 13. e3 h5 14. Bd3 e5 15. dxe5 Bxe5 16. Qc2 Kf8) (7. Bd2 { 23% } 7... g6 8. e3 Bg7 9. Be2) (7. Be5 { 15% } 7... f6 8. Bg3 Be6 9. e3 Bf7 10. Bd3 e6 11. Rc1 Be7 12. O-O Nxg3 13. hxg3 O-O) 7... Nxg3 8. hxg3 g6 9. e3 Bg7 10. Bd3 O-O 11. a3 e5) 4... Nxc6 5. d4 (5. Nc3 e5 { We have transposed into the smith morra gambit reversed, where white has already committed both knights. Next we'll go Bc5 followed by O-O, Qe7, Rd8 etc. if white doesn't fall into a trap. }) 5... e5 6. e3 (6. dxe5 Qxd1+ 7. Kxd1 Ng4 8. Ke1 g6 9. Nc3 Bg7 10. Bf4 Ngxe5 11. Nxe5 Nxe5 12. e3 O-O 13. Be2 Be6 14. Rc1) 6... exd4 7. Nxd4 Nxd4 8. Qxd4 Qxd4 9. exd4 Bb4+ 10. Bd2 Bxd2+ 11. Nxd2 O-O 12. O-O-O Bf5 *
